# deploy/microvm/microvm-worker.service
#
# Runs cmd/microvm-worker (see remote-worker/cmd/microvm-worker/main.go), the process
# that creates every VM's cgroup under microvm-vms.slice for the lifetime of the
# process (Task 17, spec §6's #1 practical failure: "Worker crash leaks VMs").
#
# WHERE EACH CGROUP ACTUALLY LANDS, because the final whole-branch review's H1 was a
# false premise about exactly this. Slice=microvm-vms.slice below makes systemd nest
# THIS UNIT's cgroup under that slice (systemd.slice(5): a unit assigned to a slice is
# placed beneath it in the cgroup tree -- the same shape as system.slice/sshd.service),
# while both launcher arms place each VM's cgroup under the same slice as a SIBLING of
# this unit, not as a child of it:
#
#   /sys/fs/cgroup/microvm-vms.slice/
#   |-- microvm-worker.service/   <-- this unit; cgroup.procs holds the worker's own pid
#   |-- vm-9/                     <-- Firecracker: jailer --parent-cgroup + --cgroup
#   `-- vm-vm-9.scope/            <-- Cloud Hypervisor: systemd-run --scope --slice=
#
# Two consequences follow, and both are load-bearing:
#
#  1. KillMode=control-group below kills what is in THIS UNIT's control group, which is
#     the worker (and any process it left in its own cgroup) -- NOT the sibling VM
#     cgroups. Tearing those down on a clean stop is `systemctl stop
#     microvm-vms.slice`, which stops the whole slice subtree. An earlier version of
#     this comment claimed KillMode=control-group reached the VM cgroups; it does not,
#     and stating it did is what made the crash-recovery sweep look optional.
#  2. Because the worker is a sibling of the VM cgroups rather than their parent,
#     SweepOrphans (called from main(), before Probe) must be able to tell them apart.
#     It does: it sweeps only directory names this pool itself created and never its
#     own cgroup (see cgroup.go's H1 block). It is the crash-recovery half of the
#     mitigation regardless -- a kill-9/OOM/power-loss leaves VM cgroups behind no
#     matter which cgroup the worker occupied.
#
# Slice=microvm-vms.slice is KEPT deliberately, not left in place by inertia: it puts
# the worker's own memory in the same accounting scope as the VMs it admits (which is
# what SH_MAX_COMMITTED_MB is reasoning about), and it makes the slice one unit an
# operator can stop or inspect to reach the whole tier. Moving the worker out would
# make "every child of the slice is a VM" true, but it would NOT remove the need for
# the sweep, and it would split the tier across two accounting scopes to buy a
# simplification the sweep no longer needs.

[Unit]
Description=microvm-worker: ephemeral microVM sandbox tier (serverless-harness P4)
Documentation=https://github.com/kagenti/serverless-harness
After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target
# Spec §6: "KVM unavailable at startup ... Fail the unit at start with an explicit
# message; never fall back to running commands on the host." AssertPathExists fails
# the unit before ExecStart even runs, which is a stronger, earlier form of that
# posture than leaving it to the process's own vmpool.New/Probe checks alone.
AssertPathExists=/dev/kvm

[Service]
Type=simple
Slice=microvm-vms.slice
ExecStart=/usr/local/bin/microvm-worker
KillMode=control-group
KillSignal=SIGTERM
TimeoutStopSec=30
Restart=on-failure
RestartSec=5s

# RLIMIT_MEMLOCK: PinMemoryFile (memfile mlock, spec §4.3) and, per VM, the guest's
# own memory need to be lockable. main() also calls vmpool.RaiseMemlockLimit() at
# startup to raise the soft limit to the hard limit within whatever this unit grants,
# but the unit's own ceiling is the hard cap that call cannot exceed -- infinity here
# is deliberate for exactly the reason spec §7.5 names: a kernel limit mistaken for a
# density ceiling fails at 500 VMs after working at 20, indistinguishably from the
# real thing, so this must not be the thing that silently caps density.
LimitMEMLOCK=infinity
# Every VM's vsock/API socket, plus the relay connection and whatever standby depth
# is configured, each cost file descriptors; spec §7.5 names nofile as one of the
# kernel limits that must be raised deliberately rather than discovered at 500 VMs.
LimitNOFILE=1048576

Environment=SH_VMM=firecracker
Environment=SH_SNAPSHOT_DIR=/srv/snapshots
Environment=SH_WORKSPACE_ROOT=/srv/workspaces
Environment=SH_CHROOT_BASE=/srv/jail
Environment=SH_PARENT_CGROUP=/sys/fs/cgroup/microvm-vms.slice
Environment=SH_STANDBY_DEPTH=2
Environment=SH_GUEST_RAM_MB=256
Environment=SH_MAX_RUNS=64
Environment=SH_MAX_COMMITTED_MB=24576
Environment=SH_MEMORY_RESERVE_MB=4096

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
